## Phase 8: Capture ocean classification and suppress ocean overlays

Depends on Phases 3–7.

Outcome: explored ocean columns receive neither habitat highlights nor unavailable crosshatching, using the terrain layer's underlying surface-block category rather than an elevation cutoff or rendered pixel colors. Freshwater is outside this exclusion.

- [x] Verify the installed `ChunkMapLayer.GenerateChunkImage` surface selection, `RainHeightMap`, `BlockLayersAccess.FluidOrSolid`, snow handling, and public `block2Color`/`colorsByCode` lookup. Use the resolved `ocean` category, including modded blocks assigned that category; do not hardcode saltwater block IDs, RGB values or below-sea-level thresholds. Ocean, lake and lava can share an RGB color.
- [x] Select and verify a narrow acquisition hook that captures the semantic classification before shoreline decoration/shading. Inspect whether the native process can be observed directly; avoid reproducing its full pixel-generation loop or invoking map image generation solely to obtain ocean flags. Define behavior for color-accurate maps and native-hook incompatibility. Keep version-sensitive access in an owning adapter.
- [x] Acquire immutable per-column ocean flags only from normally available loaded voxel data, with stable chunk/column coordinates and safe thread/unload ownership. Cover initial loaded areas, normal arrivals and tab-closed capture. Do not perform voxel reads on the render thread, load or generate remote chunks, query vanilla's database, or infer classification from saved map images.
- [x] Persist a compact ocean mask with tile-specific availability/provenance in the feature-owned database. Version serialization/completeness as needed, retain otherwise valid environmental inputs, and define explicit behavior for records lacking the new component. Missing classification must remain distinguishable from confirmed land; do not add backfill or fabricate flags for old cached tiles. Reacquire through normal play.
- [x] Extend immutable preparation bundles, source invalidation and graphics-thread texture ownership to carry ocean classification and its availability. Reuse these inputs across species changes and integrate binding with the shared graphics pass; preserve purge epochs, stale-receipt rejection, close/reopen and shutdown/flush contracts.
- [x] Make the GPU discard confirmed ocean columns before habitat comparison or unavailable crosshatching. Preserve explored clipping, existing missing-input semantics for non-ocean columns and freshwater behavior. Keep land below sea level eligible under its actual habitat ranges; preserve islands and shoreline land columns.
- [x] Add focused checks for native category lookup, modded ocean blocks, saltwater versus freshwater/lava despite identical colors, shoreline decoration, surface layering, missing classification, persistence/reopen and invalidation. Verify capture and presentation behavior through the normal map/RenderDoc workflow, including known oceans with other environmental components missing. Do not introduce a standalone graphics harness or permanent diagnostic readbacks.
- [x] Use subagents for focused tests and canonical build/package checks. Reconcile the proposal and current acquisition/storage/evaluation documentation with the implemented classification, availability and lifecycle contracts. Record user-run verification and leave broader resource/performance qualification to Phase 10.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Source-backed acquisition, persistence, GPU suppression and normal-map evidence establish that ocean columns remain unmarked, including when other environmental data is unavailable. Non-ocean eligibility, freshwater behavior, explored clipping and lifecycle contracts remain intact; missing classification stays explicit, no backfill/base-database access is introduced, and linked documentation/build receipts are current.

Implementation and source evidence: the inspected installed VSEssentials binary still matches Integration contract 1's SHA-256. `ChunkMapLayer.GenerateChunkImage` uses `RainHeightMap`, a fully loaded client column, `FluidOrSolid` reads and one snow step before color/shoreline decoration. `WorldChunk.UnpackAndReadBlock` takes its unpack lock; `ChunkData.GetBlockId` layer 3 prioritizes fluid over solid. The image loop itself is map-open dependent and has no standalone category operation, so `NativeOceanClassifier` captures only this small surface-selection boundary using the native resolved public category table. It does not generate map images or copy shading/decoration. Canonical parchment surface semantics apply in color-accurate mode; incompatible/missing native lookup leaves classification unavailable.

Traceability: tasks 1–3 map to the proposal's ocean/acquisition scope and [EnvironmentalAcquisition.md](EnvironmentalAcquisition.md#ocean-surface-classification). Native category/layer/snow selection, mod-assigned ocean categories despite identical colors, missing/disposed surfaces, initial inventory, normal packet/dirty arrivals and tab-closed worker capture have focused evidence. Coordinate-only intake coalesces up to 128 pending keys plus one overflow inventory obligation; native reads occur on one worker under receipt/unload synchronization, outside event/render callbacks and before bounded write admission. A second review and independent audit identified and resolved a delayed-callback race: intake now rejects old epochs and preserves newer pending/overflow ordinals. The regression covers reordered callbacks and post-purge stale callbacks.

Tasks 4–6 map to [EnvironmentalStorage.md](EnvironmentalStorage.md#persistent-records), [HabitatEvaluation.md](HabitatEvaluation.md#gpu-sampling-and-output), and proposal persistence/evaluation/lifecycle requirements. Tile blob format 2 carries independently available elevations and a two-bit ocean grid without changing the schema or opening vanilla's database. Existing format-1 elevations remain usable with unknown classification. Independent component ordering/merge, compact encoding, reopen, purge rejection and tile lease invalidation are tested. Raw categories share the existing R8UI coverage texture, retaining five samplers and the shared-pass state boundary. The shader clips unexplored columns, then discards confirmed ocean before habitat sampling or unavailable hatching; unknown classification remains explicit, while freshwater and below-sea-level land retain ordinary eligibility. Ocean-only bundles remain usable even when heights/climate are absent. Shutdown joins ocean intake before stopping receipt capture and draining durable writes.

