## Phase 9.1: Select and implement built-in lossless database compression

Outcome: ecosystem environmental array payloads in the dedicated database use a measured choice of built-in .NET Deflate or Brotli compression, preserving every original value without adding a compression-library dependency.

- [x] Compare DeflateStream and BrotliStream on representative regional climate/forest/shrub arrays and chunk height/ocean data, including small, uniform and varied payloads. Use identical serialized inputs and record compressed size, encode/decode time and allocation costs at suitable compression levels. Use subagents for benchmark/test tools; keep these CPU/storage measurements separate from live rendering performance.
- [x] Select and document the codec and compression level based on the measured storage/read/write tradeoffs before implementing the production path. Keep this decision independent of the GPU packing and atlas work in 9.2–9.3.
- [x] Refactor the database writer to compress serialized array payloads using the selected built-in codec. Retain coordinates, dimensions, padding, component availability and source identity; compression must not quantize or otherwise change the original values. Preserve existing queued-write ordering and shutdown durability.
- [x] Refactor the database reader to decompress and validate payloads before constructing environmental records. Define the current format/version and enforce expected decoded lengths and bounded output; handle malformed or truncated records through the existing unavailable-data behavior rather than accepting partial arrays or fabricated values.
- [x] DO NOT implement data migration, legacy readers or backfill. Existing incompatible feature-cache records remain unavailable under the version policy until ordinary acquisition replaces them; do not access vanilla's database or request old regions.
- [x] Verify exact round trips for all supported component layouts, integer extrema, flags and independently missing components, plus malformed input, database reopen and pending-write shutdown. Use subagents for required tests and canonical build/package checks. Reconcile permanent storage documentation with the implemented format and record measured results and limitations.

Completion criteria:

- [x] A built-in codec and compression level are selected with recorded comparative measurements; the production writer/reader use that choice without an external compression dependency.
- [x] Supported environmental payloads round-trip exactly, validation rejects corrupt data safely, and persistence/lifecycle checks and canonical packaging pass. No migration or legacy handling is introduced.

### Compression decision and verification

Task-to-document traceability: all six tasks inherit the proposal's dedicated persistence, original-value preservation, partial-input and no-backfill contracts, plus Integration contract 7. Tasks 1–2 use the comparison below; tasks 3–5 map to [EnvironmentalStorage.md](EnvironmentalStorage.md#persistent-records) and its ordering/lifetime section. Task 6 also maps to [EnvironmentalAcquisition.md](EnvironmentalAcquisition.md#payload-and-routing) for unchanged raw shrub delivery and [HabitatEvaluation.md](HabitatEvaluation.md#ownership-and-validity) for storage epochs/source invalidation. The proposal, storage, acquisition, GPU ownership documentation and relevant SQLite connection source were consulted before implementation. No GPU packing or atlas work is included.

Selection: built-in DeflateStream at CompressionLevel.Optimal. A TEMP-only net10.0 Release comparison linked the actual raw serializer/models and checked exact round trips for 23 deterministic payloads (seed 17391): climate 20×20 with padding 2/2, forest 17×17 and shrubs 33×33 with padding 0/1, small 3×3 regional maps, and 32×32 height/ocean tiles. Each included uniform, smooth, varied and full-range noise cases, plus height-only, ocean-only and empty tile records. Twenty warm-ups preceded five batches of 100 operations; results below sum each workload's median batch cost for one operation. Serialization is excluded; stream/buffer allocations are included. Managed allocations exclude native compressor memory. Equal weighting is synthetic, not a real-world distribution.

| Codec / level | Compressed bytes | Encode sum | Decode sum | Managed encode / decode bytes |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Deflate Fastest | 21,618 | 158.339 µs | 67.403 µs | 52,953 / 48,288 |
| Deflate Optimal | 16,474 | 365.415 µs | 55.336 µs | 43,064 / 48,288 |
| Deflate SmallestSize | 15,886 | 470.614 µs | 56.510 µs | 42,008 / 48,288 |
| Brotli Fastest | 17,295 | 152.555 µs | 86.757 µs | 75,610 / 45,346 |
| Brotli Optimal | 16,094 | 496.080 µs | 79.376 µs | 40,513 / 45,345 |
| Brotli SmallestSize | 14,923 | 30,972.138 µs | 105.941 µs | 38,219 / 45,347 |

Original aggregate: 40,544 bytes. The production 20-byte envelope adds 460 bytes across these 23 blobs, making the selected total 16,934 bytes. Deflate Optimal balances low measured read cost with moderate encoding cost; Brotli Optimal saves only 380 bytes before equal envelope overhead, while Deflate SmallestSize saves 588 bytes with more encoding work. An independent process repeat preserved sizes/allocations and supported the same choice despite timing variability (Deflate Optimal 444.069/67.275 µs encode/decode sums, Brotli Optimal 543.875/87.972 µs). Uniform/smooth inputs shrink substantially; full-range noise and tiny records can grow. No per-record size guarantee, SQLite throughput result or live frame-time improvement is claimed.

Measurement environment: .NET 10.0.13, Windows 10.0.26200, Intel Family 6 Model 151 Stepping 2. Subagent commands: dotnet run --project "$env:TEMP/ve-ecosystem-compression-20261020/Benchmark.csproj" -c Release -p:UseSharedCompilation=false, followed by the same project with -c Release --no-build. Temporary sources and complete results are retained locally as results.txt and results-repeat.txt; they are not repository dependencies. Measurements exclude the production envelope/checksum, database/disk I/O and all GPU work.

Implementation: database-only EnvironmentalCompression preserves raw byte payloads using a versioned, length-checked envelope, bounded decompression and an FNV-1a accidental-corruption checksum. Regional/tile database row versions are 2/3; schema and shrub wire versions are unchanged. Legacy tile decoding is removed. Existing queue, merge, transaction, purge and shutdown owners remain in place. Focused checks cover integer extrema/native layouts, all tile component combinations, malformed envelopes, component isolation, incompatible-row reacquisition and unchanged shrub wire bytes. Second source review rechecked format boundaries, exact/bounded decode, partial merges, removal of legacy decoding, acquisition isolation and unchanged queue/purge/shutdown ownership. Subagent validation passes 103 focused EcosystemMap tests and 2,169 full-suite tests using dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore -p:UseSharedCompilation=false (focused filter FullyQualifiedName~EcosystemMap). Canonical ./build.ps1 --configuration=Release passes JSON validation/build/package with VINTAGE_STORY=G:/Vintagestory, NUGET_PACKAGES=C:/Users/Sisco/.nuget/packages and game Lib on PATH. Existing unrelated C# and Cake Pkcs warnings remain. ZIP Releases/vanillaexpanded_999.99.9.zip: 1,981,988 bytes, SHA-256 397F171F2F9BA7F53C36685D50F71CCAEF45EC495FDCA46816DC6C256EEEA599. Packaged DLL matches Release output (SHA-256 0E590683DBB554C3DFA8510A52E74295D128633BF71A4D3BD58AD99340DD7643); both shaders match unchanged source, project dependencies are unchanged and no compression library is added. The standard Install development mod task includes this implementation. The independent audit directly reconstructed governing links, consulted source/tests and both benchmark runs, and corroborated validation with the build/test owner. It found no confirmed defect or unresolved evidence gap in this storage-only contract. Phase 9.1 is complete; 9.2, 9.3 and 10 remain pending. No live rendering or storage-throughput result is claimed.

