## Phase 9.3: Atlas packed GPU inputs

Depends on Phase 9.2 and the existing incremental view-retention contracts.

Outcome: regional and terrain inputs occupy stable rectangles in two bounded atlas resources, reducing texture-object switches while preserving source reuse and rendering behavior.

- [x] Evaluate the base game's provided texture atlas type and allocation APIs against the installed implementation before selecting an owner. Check feature-owned lifetime, RGBA8 UNORM/RG16UI support, rectangular allocation/release, partial uploads, growth or relocation, coordinate updates, padding/filtering, graphics-thread requirements and hardware limits. Record source-backed suitability; assess reuse of native allocation facilities separately if its texture upload path requires color formats. Use a narrow feature-owned alternative only where the native facilities cannot satisfy the contract, without modifying unrelated game atlases.
- [x] Implement separate regional and terrain atlas ownership using the packed formats from 9.2. Allocate each regional source once for all dependent tiles and retain independent channel-layout metadata alongside atlas addresses. Retain exact integer terrain addressing and the regional UNORM filtering/corner-selection rules; prevent neighboring-slot or mip contamination and preserve texel-center coordinates within each allocation.
- [x] Bound atlas capacity and metadata using hardware limits and measured workloads. Protect the visible explored working set, evict off-view allocations first, and define explicit page/capacity behavior when two physical textures cannot accommodate that working set. Bind the two atlases once per pass when it fits; document any page-driven rebinding rather than promising unlimited single-atlas capacity.
- [x] Upload only new or changed rectangles. Preserve retained allocations across pan, zoom and species changes; do not rebuild the atlas or reread unchanged sources on view changes. Coordinate growth, relocation and recycled slots with source leases and draw receipts so stale work cannot sample another source's allocation.
- [x] Integrate atlas addresses and binding ownership with the shared graphics pass, targeted invalidation, coverage gates, shader reload, map close/reopen, purge and world shutdown. Cached rectangles must not expose unexplored areas or bypass ocean/missing-data behavior. Keep draw batching or instancing in Phase 9.9, separate from atlas allocation and addressing.
- [x] Add focused regressions for allocation reuse, partial updates, growth/relocation, recycled-slot rejection, capacity handling, overlapping views and resource disposal. Use subagents for build/test tools and canonical packaging, then obtain normal user-run map verification and capture evidence for binding reduction and stable rendering.
- [x] Measure texture objects, binding/upload counts, atlas occupancy, GPU payload and CPU metadata costs on representative zoomed-out views. Keep binding improvements distinct from residual per-tile draw costs and original live frame-time evidence; reconcile implemented architecture and proposal documentation.

Completion criteria:

- [x] The native-atlas reuse decision is supported by source inspection, packed atlas sampling is correct, retained tiles remain stable and resource bounds/lifetimes are verified. Captures demonstrate expected binding behavior, user-run rendering passes, and measured costs and remaining performance limits are recorded.

### Atlas implementation and verification (2026-10-20)

Task-to-document traceability: every task inherits the proposal's GPU-only comparison, dedicated persistence, full-map/coverage and lifecycle boundaries. Task 1 maps to installed TextureAtlas/TextureAtlasNode/TextureAtlasManager, ITextureAtlasAPI and TextureAtlasPosition; tasks 2–5 map to [HabitatEvaluation.md](HabitatEvaluation.md#atlas-allocation-and-sampling), its sampling/shared-pass/retention sections and the Phase 9.2 representation contract. Tasks 4–5 also retain [EnvironmentalStorage.md](EnvironmentalStorage.md#ordering-and-lifetime) and [EnvironmentalAcquisition.md](EnvironmentalAcquisition.md#purge-and-exit). Tasks 6–7 and completion inherit normal game/capture and measured-cost requirements, the proposal's performance/validation expectations and the same architecture sources. These sources were consulted before implementation and independently rechecked against the current scope.

Native reuse decision: installed public TextureAtlas can reserve fixed rows independently of its bitmap. NativeAtlasLayout uses logical slot units, immediately calls DisposePixels and reserves rows once; generation-stamped cell reuse avoids per-tile native tree searches. The native manager requires ClientMain and controls BGRA uploads/global positions/mipmaps, so feature-owned GL pages supply RGBA8 UNORM/RG16UI storage and partial TexSubImage2D transfers. No unrelated game atlas, database, dependency, wire format or environmental record format changes. Pages remain immutable; expansion adds pages instead of relocating retained allocations. Empty pages trim on maintenance, partially occupied pages remain until their sources leave, and source LRUs retain off-view-first eviction. Retention plus one transient replacement slot bounds live leases. The provisional axis ceiling is 4,096 subject to actual hardware limits, with extra pages for overflow or differing source-square sizes; this is not a final memory-budget acceptance.

Shader sampling adds independent regional/terrain pixel origins. Regional sampling checks original logical bounds before applying the atlas offset. Hardware filtering requires a full texel of allocation margin; allocation edges and non-adjacent worldgen corners use exact texelFetch interpolation, preventing neighboring-slot access without graphics-thread packing or guard-buffer construction. Terrain keeps exact unsigned integer addressing. Draw submission remains per tile; each sampler rebinds only on first use or a page change, and bindings reset each shared pass. Worker packing/decompression and persisted packed inputs remain unchanged. Upload state is still preserved per operation, with batching reserved for 9.4.

Second source review and independent audit found no confirmed source/lifecycle/capacity issue. The audit's initial atlas edge-isolation evidence gap was resolved with the strict-interior/exact-edge shader strategy and independently rechecked. Focused tests exercise native row bounds, page overflow, retained-address reuse, recycled-generation rejection, partial-transfer rollback, clear/disposal, page-aware bindings and existing source-keyed LRU retention. Recording-page tests show two sampler bindings for a shared-page draw sequence and only the terrain sampler rebinding on terrain-page overflow. These are production binding API calls observed without GL, not captured driver results.

A TEMP-only non-graphics experiment links the exact native-layout production source and uses the historical 7,510-terrain/86-region workload. Across five Release warm runs, layout construction and allocation of all cells allocate 144,072 managed bytes including transient native allocator data; elapsed times are 0.0664, 0.1207, 0.0765, 0.0678 and 0.0718 ms (median 0.0718 ms). This measures allocator work only, excluding live source caches, leases, graphics owner metadata and GL. The resulting 32-pixel terrain page is 2,784² with 7,569 slots (7,510 occupied), and the 33-pixel regional page is 330×297 with 90 slots (86 occupied). Combined format-derived page payload is 31,394,664 bytes across two textures, excluding driver overhead; no GPU allocation or frame-time measurement is claimed. Mixed layouts, partial retention and incremental page growth can produce different occupancy/page counts. Current capture/resource and scoped CPU metadata evidence below supplements this earlier layout-only experiment; broader frame-time and whole-feature memory acceptance remain separate.

Final candidate validation: subagent-run dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore -p:UseSharedCompilation=false --filter FullyQualifiedName~EcosystemMap passes 122/122; the unfiltered suite passes 2,188/2,188. Canonical ./build.ps1 --configuration=Release passes JSON validation/build/package with VINTAGE_STORY=G:/Vintagestory, NUGET_PACKAGES=C:/Users/Sisco/.nuget/packages and game Lib on PATH. Existing unrelated compiler and Cake Pkcs warnings remain. ZIP SHA-256 DB97C711CC967B754A8A68AFBE3A200E18F4C3B23655584E7785726429833CBC (1,993,368 bytes, 153 entries). Release/publish/packaged DLLs match SHA-256 8721FD777194AFDAE9972EBF65B36ECC6B46F3F54047275B5256C8DFAF1185D4. Fragment source/ZIP match DE582D0998A47C6562DA75A8C4BE5EE1C904D1A5FAC805C47FEF6A7E2694400F; vertex remains F5AE13F5E8CD8F88BF018A757B36B6F668C86AF20F2FFABE741320861B8FDA3E. The normal Install development mod task includes these changes; no game was launched.

User-run visual verification (2026-10-20): in response to the normal Install development mod check covering highlights/crosshatching, species switching, panning, zooming and close/reopen, the user confirms "yes, it is corect". client-debug.log line 26 identifies the feature-worktree Debug DLL loaded at 13:58:59 America/Los_Angeles, and client-main.log line 402 records successful ecosystem_habitat shader loading at 14:06:20. The earlier old-package/old-capture evidence is superseded by the fresh capture below.

