## Phase 9.4: Batch texture-upload state handling

Depends on the prepacked upload-buffer path; coordinate ownership with Phase 9.3's atlas implementation.

Outcome: graphics state needed for uploading is captured, configured and restored once per upload batch rather than once per texture.

- [x] Measure current upload-state queries and changes separately from texture allocation and transfer costs. Cache immutable hardware limits at graphics-owner initialization.
- [x] Introduce one scoped upload batch around newly ready resources. Preserve unpack alignment, row length, row/pixel skips, pixel-unpack-buffer and texture bindings; restore once on success or exception. Keep allocation/upload on the graphics thread and all packing/decompression on workers.
- [x] Integrate both current texture ownership and atlas rectangle uploads without allocations or uploads inside the shared draw pass. Retain source validity checks and safe partial-failure cleanup.
- [x] Verify state restoration and lifecycle behavior with focused checks, subagent-run build/tests and canonical packaging. Compare upload-state call counts; the user waived the live acquisition-cost comparison as recorded below.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Upload batches preserve caller state and resource validity, repeated per-resource state setup is removed, and verified state-call counts remain distinct from allocation/transfer costs; the waived live timing comparison is not claimed as performed.

### Upload-batch implementation and verification (2026-10-20)

Task-to-document traceability: all tasks inherit the proposal's graphics-thread resource ownership, GPU-only evaluation and persistence/lifecycle boundaries. Task 1 maps to baseline GpuHabitatTexture.WithUploadState and the current graphics-owner initialization; tasks 2–3 map to [HabitatEvaluation.md](HabitatEvaluation.md#atlas-allocation-and-sampling), its shared-pass/ownership sections, Phase 9.3's immutable-page/source-validity contracts and the proposal's performance/lifecycle expectations. [EnvironmentalStorage.md](EnvironmentalStorage.md#ordering-and-lifetime) and [EnvironmentalAcquisition.md](EnvironmentalAcquisition.md#purge-and-exit) retain worker/durable-write/purge guarantees. Task 4 and completion inherit the same sources and the explicit user waiver of the live comparison below; automated state-call evidence does not substitute for live acquisition timing.

Baseline: subagent-run focused tests pass 122/122 before changes. The exact pre-change texture source was preserved in TEMP for review. Every page allocation and every rectangle transfer independently issues six GL state queries, five setup calls (one pixel-unpack-buffer bind and four pixel-store calls), and six restoration calls (four pixel-store calls, one buffer bind and one texture bind), in addition to its actual allocation/transfer/target-binding operations. GL_MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE was already queried once in HabitatMapGraphics initialization; that cached ownership is retained. The available 9.3 capture has zero actual-frame uploads; earlier pre-atlas profile samples are not an equivalent upload-active baseline. Current normal client logs do not contain applicable upload timing. A normal baseline profiling request was sent before asking the user to install the new candidate.

Implementation: GpuHabitatUploads owns one non-nested graphics-thread batch around the complete ready-resource Prepare loop. Opening a batch does no GL work; the first page allocation or transfer captures six caller values and establishes contiguous CPU-array transfer state. Both regional and terrain atlas factories share the same coordinator. GpuHabitatTexture requires that scope before allocation/transfer, retains native TexImage2D/TexSubImage2D and buffer pinning, and contains no per-operation state wrapper or channel packing. The scope restores alignment, row length, row/pixel skips, pixel-unpack-buffer and current-unit 2D texture binding before any draw-pass setup, including on preparation exceptions. Native texture operations never change the active texture unit. The shared draw pass, source checks, atlas slots/pages, worker packing/decompression and persisted records remain independent.

The narrow IHabitatUploadApi/NativeHabitatUploadApi boundary permits focused state restoration tests without a graphics context. Tests exercise nondefault caller values, one versus 250 operations in one batch, zero-work/cache-only batches, failed setup before native allocation, operation failure and subsequent reuse, nested/unscoped misuse, stale-scope disposal and foreign-thread disposal. A real presentation regression verifies one scope around both ready resources, scope end before draw setup and restoration on failed acquisition. There are six queries/five setup/six restore calls per active batch (17 state-management calls total), and zero for an unused batch. For 250 operations, the source baseline is 1,500 queries/1,250 setup/1,500 restore calls versus 6/5/6 in one batch. Actual page allocation, texture parameters, target binding, pinning and transfer calls are separate and unchanged; these call counts do not measure driver stalls or live frame time.

Second source review and independent completion audit reconcile lazy ownership, error cleanup, worker boundaries, native calls, tests and permanent architecture/proposal wording with no confirmed implementation issue. The original audit supported tasks 1–3 and identified only the missing live comparison; the user subsequently waived that gate.

Subagent validation: dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore -p:UseSharedCompilation=false --filter FullyQualifiedName~EcosystemMap passes 130/130; the unfiltered suite passes 2,196/2,196. Canonical ./build.ps1 --configuration=Release passes JSON validation/build/package with the standard VINTAGE_STORY=G:/Vintagestory, NUGET_PACKAGES=C:/Users/Sisco/.nuget/packages and game Lib on PATH. Existing unrelated compiler and Cake Pkcs warnings remain. ZIP SHA-256 E994CC70C35538B0575EEBDD9F5D936D15063EB5A0CE49F564E9434BB4551143 (1,994,016 bytes, 153 entries); Release/publish/packaged DLLs match 9DB4F738B0C6CD40B530797F5768DA24782B275CC8B9C3418D2FA289120A5716. Both habitat shaders remain unchanged and match source/ZIP. Dependencies, manifest and task configuration are unchanged; the normal Install development mod task includes the candidate. No game was launched or custom installer used.

User-directed waiver (2026-10-20): the user answered "no, just proceed without this" to the request for baseline profiling and a before/after live acquisition-cost comparison. That specific timing gate is waived, not recorded as performed. The existing 9.3 capture has no actual-frame uploads and remains unsuitable for that comparison. No driver-time, hitch reduction or overall frame-time speedup is claimed from adapter call counts. Later upload budgeting and integrated performance validation remain separate unchecked work.

Completion: the independent affected-requirement audit accepts the explicit user waiver and finds no remaining Phase 9.4 implementation or evidence gap under the revised contract. Phase 9.4 is complete on the source review, state-call/restoration checks, full validation and canonical package evidence above. Later phases remain unchecked.

## Phase 9.5: Bound graphics-thread uploads per frame

Depends on Phase 9.4; preserve the atlas and view-retention contracts when integrating Phase 9.3.

Outcome: entering a large explored view cannot submit an unlimited burst of new texture uploads in one frame.

- [x] Measure upload bytes/counts and graphics-thread elapsed time on representative map opening and zoom changes. Choose a documented per-frame admission budget using that evidence, including progress for a resource larger than the normal byte budget.
- [x] Defer excess upload-ready receipts while continuing to draw valid resident resources. Prioritize current visible work, retain bounded pending ownership and recheck source leases, coverage and session lifetime before publication.
- [x] Treat regional and terrain readiness coherently so partially admitted resources cannot produce invalid draws. Purge, close and shutdown must discard deferred disposable work safely without blocking rendering or durable storage writes.
- [x] Verify bounded admission, eventual progress, view changes and cancellation; use subagents for build/tests and packaging. Obtain user-run checks and measure frame hitches separately from total time until the view is populated.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Admission obeys the selected budget with documented oversized-resource behavior, resident tiles stay drawable, and live evidence records both frame smoothness and loading latency.

Implementation and verification notes (2026-10-21):

- Task/document traceability: budget selection and deferred rendering follow `EcosystemMapProposal.md#performance-and-lifecycle-expectations` and `HabitatEvaluation.md` (Upload admission; atlas allocation and sampling). `HabitatUploadBudget`, `HabitatAtlas.EstimateUpload`, and `HabitatMapGraphics.TryPrepare` account for coherent transfer/page-allocation costs before publication. Provisional defaults: 256 KiB, 64 native image operations, 2 ms soft elapsed stop; one first oversized admission guarantees progress. Live calibration was not performed; its completion gate is waived below.
- Lifecycle tasks follow `EnvironmentalStorage.md` (Ordering and lifetime) and `EnvironmentalAcquisition.md` (Purge and exit): existing source leases, coverage and session epochs gate publication; deferred ownership adds only one priority coordinate. `HabitatMapPresentation` keeps resident draws and clears priority on suspension without cancelling durable writes.
- Focused subagent validation: 141/141 ecosystem tests passed (baseline 130/130). Added budget/count/time, allocation forecasting, coherent readiness, oversized progress, view-priority/removal, suspension and purge checks. Full subagent validation: 2,207/2,207 tests passed; canonical Release JSON validation/build/package passed. ZIP SHA-256 `AC11C2EC60AAEDFF628E81A28E1A9FB2824CF8794487082803EA0F1AC0F67013`; Release/publish/packaged DLLs match. Standard Install development mod includes the candidate. Second source review and independent completion audit found no confirmed implementation defect. The user subsequently supplied live profile logs and reported improved but still laggy rendering. Remaining calibration and loading-latency statistics were explicitly waived below.

Live evidence (2026-10-22): the user reports rendering remains laggy but improved. `client-main.log` through 07:59:26 local contains 626 ecosystem profile samples: median ecosystem 54.85 ms, draw-and-restore 34.56 ms (622 samples), texture-acquisition 6.78 ms (621 samples), input-preparation 6.67 ms, tile-placement 2.39 ms and view-membership 0.81 ms. The last 100 samples have median ecosystem 66.84 ms and draw-and-restore 43.74 ms. Overall profiled-frame median is 95.155 ms; logging overhead and workload changes preclude a controlled before/after speedup claim. No ecosystem/shader errors were found. Acquisition includes cache/currentness work and possible uploads, so these marks cannot isolate upload bytes/counts or driver time. Draw submission is the largest measured component; recurring processing and draw batching remain covered by 9.7/9.9. Upload calibration and total population latency remain unverified.

User-directed waiver (2026-10-22): the user requested closing 9.5 without the remaining statistics. Isolated upload byte/operation counts, live graphics-thread budget calibration and total view-population latency are waived as completion gates, not recorded as measured. Retain the documented provisional limits and oversized-resource behavior; no empirically optimal budget or controlled speedup is claimed. The reported improvement and residual drawing cost are recorded above. Remaining recurring processing and draw batching stay in 9.7/9.9.

Completion: the independent affected-requirement recheck accepts the explicit 2026-10-22 waiver and finds 9.5 satisfied under the revised contract. Upload bounds, coherent readiness, resident draws and lifecycle behavior are supported by source review and focused checks; full validation and canonical packaging passed. Unperformed measurements are not claimed as verified. Later phases remain unchecked.

## Phase 9.6: Batch local database reads

Depends on the persisted packed-buffer path. Preserve single-worker ownership of the dedicated ecosystem database.

Outcome: bounded groups of needed packed records share queue/SQL work instead of requiring an independent command submission for every tile.

- [ ] Measure command/queue overhead separately from disk reads and Deflate decoding. Select bounded batch sizes from representative workloads; do not create speculative remote retrieval or another database connection.
- [ ] Add bounded multi-tile packed reads with deduplicated regional dependencies, parameterized queries and reusable commands where supported. Return per-record missing-data results and preserve each tile's region association and format validation.
- [ ] Integrate preparation admission, source leases, targeted invalidation and purge epochs. Allow accepted source writes and shutdown/purge barriers to progress between bounded read batches; never wait for database results on the graphics thread.
- [ ] Verify partial batches, shared regions, malformed/missing records, updates during reads and purge/shutdown behavior. Run build/tests and packaging through subagents; compare command counts, allocations and time until visible data becomes ready.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Batch reads reduce measured submission/SQL overhead without unbounded work, stale publication, write starvation or any access to vanilla's database.

## Phase 9.7: Reduce recurring view and receipt processing

Depends on the existing incremental retention and source-validity contracts; reconcile with Phase 9.5's deferred uploads.

Outcome: stable and changing views avoid unnecessary collection allocation and repeated per-tile CPU work.

- [ ] Profile view-set construction, coverage/placement collection creation, receipt/cache lookup and validity checks separately on stable and changing views. Identify duplicated work before changing ownership.
- [ ] Reuse owned temporary collections and avoid rebuilding unchanged membership where verified inputs permit it. Continue updating screen positions with pan/zoom; do not confuse unchanged chunk membership with unchanged placement.
- [ ] Consolidate redundant cache/currentness checks only where invalidation cannot intervene. Retain checks at asynchronous completion, graphics publication and draw boundaries that protect against changed source leases, coverage and purge/session epochs.
- [ ] Verify overlapping views, coverage removal, source updates, species changes and lifecycle transitions. Use subagents for build/tests and packaging; compare per-frame allocations and CPU ranges on equivalent zoomed-out workloads.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Measured recurring allocations/CPU work decrease while current coverage, source freshness and stable overlapping-tile behavior remain intact.

## Phase 9.8: Make packed persistence authoritative

Depends on the prepacked worker/storage path. Coordinate database readers with Phase 9.6 and preserve independent component arrivals.

Outcome: the feature database persists the packed environmental representation without also storing duplicate original regional channel payloads.

- [ ] Trace all original-record consumers and define the authoritative packed record contract before replacing them. Preserve per-channel size, padding, offsets, availability and independently arriving climate/forest/shrub data. Document intentional removal of climate's unused low byte; do not narrow heights or vegetation densities further.
- [ ] Merge incoming regional channels into the authoritative packed representation on the storage worker, realigning preserved channels when layouts change without resampling or evaluating habitat. Evaluate the same duplicate-storage removal for terrain height/flag records and retain exact values and coordinate association.
- [ ] Keep Deflate compression, dedicated database identity, atomic updates, targeted notifications and purge/shutdown durability. Define an explicit incompatible-cache version/reset policy; do not add legacy decoders, backfill or world-data retrieval. Missing knowledge must remain unavailable.
- [ ] Remove superseded original/derived dual-write and reconstruction paths once all consumers use the authoritative records. Update permanent architecture documentation to reflect the resulting format and ownership.
- [ ] Verify arrival order, partial updates, layout changes, missing/invalid records, reopen, purge and accepted-write shutdown. Use subagents for build/tests and canonical packaging; measure database size and write/merge work separately from normal packed-read latency.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Duplicate environmental persistence is removed with preserved habitat inputs and lifecycle guarantees; measured storage/write effects and the cache compatibility policy are recorded without claiming an unmeasured loading-speed gain.

## Phase 9.9: Batch atlas draw submissions

Depends on Phase 9.3. Coordinate with upload budgeting and preserve the shared graphics pass.

Outcome: compatible tiles share draw submissions where atlas addressing and the native map rendering API permit it.

- [ ] Inspect installed engine mesh/instancing APIs and measure residual per-tile submission overhead after atlas integration. Choose bounded combined geometry or instancing from source-backed feasibility and representative measurements.
- [ ] Carry tile placement, world origin, regional/terrain atlas addresses and independent regional layouts through the chosen geometry/instance representation. Keep the selected criteria uniforms shared and all habitat comparisons on the GPU.
- [ ] Group only compatible atlas pages and state. Preserve native GUI depth/scissor/order, transparent discards, ocean suppression, unavailable crosshatching and current explored coverage. Reuse geometry where valid and reject stale or recycled allocations before submission.
- [ ] Verify view/zoom changes, page changes, updates and resource lifetimes through focused checks and subagent-run build/tests/package. Obtain user-run visual checks and captures comparing draw counts, CPU submission time and GPU cost; do not equate fewer draws with proven frame-time improvement.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Compatible tile draws are batched with preserved rendering and validity behavior, and captures and measured costs support the chosen submission design.

## Phase 10: Validate the integrated feature and reconcile documentation

Depends on Phases 1–9, including subphases 9.1–9.9.

Outcome: completion is supported by separate automated, packaged, performance, and live visual evidence.

- [ ] Use subagents for required build/test tools, following repository instructions. Record exact commands, target version, results, and limitations; resolve failures before marking related work complete.
- [ ] Verify client/server packaging and registration using the repository's existing workflow, including shrub-delivery support and operation of unrelated features when that support is absent.
- [ ] Exercise the integrated lifecycle across redraw, map close/reopen, unload, reconnect, purge, version changes, normal shutdown with pending writes, and server/world switches. Confirm ecosystem storage never accesses vanilla's database.
- [ ] Measure map opening, panning, species switching, environmental capture, shrub delivery, CPU cache size and GPU resource usage on representative explored maps. Include view/selection costs and texture reuse; record workloads and measured results, and adjust bounds based on evidence before claiming suitable limits or frame-time performance.
- [ ] Investigate and resolve the severe zoom-out lag reported during the 2026-10-20 tile-retention check. Measure the affected zoom/view workload and distinguish CPU frame work, local input preparation, texture allocation/upload and GPU drawing costs before attributing a cause or choosing a fix. Recheck normal pan/zoom behavior after any change; retain the existing source-reuse, coverage and lifecycle contracts. The user confirmed retention correctness, not acceptable performance.
- [ ] Obtain user-run in-game acceptance for readable terrain, dropdown visibility and selection retention, stable habitat highlighting, and red crosshatching in mixed-coverage views. Do not launch the game on the user's behalf for visual acceptance.
- [ ] Confirm live disappearance of crosshatching as inputs arrive; confirm no overlay on unexplored terrain, minimap, or inactive tab, including after full-map close. Confirm old uncached areas remain unavailable until normally revisited.
- [ ] Update permanent feature documentation to describe implemented behavior, missing-data presentation, server support, dedicated cache, and no-backfill limits. Keep historical/staging details in planning evidence only.
- [ ] Reconcile the implementation and completion receipts against every proposal requirement and this checklist. Keep unmet gates unchecked; report blockers rather than replacing required runtime evidence with headless checks.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Build/test and package receipts, measured performance evidence, and user-run visual acceptance are separately recorded and satisfactory; no required gate remains pending.

### Validation and remaining evidence (2026-10-20)

Task-to-document traceability: all tasks inherit this checklist's boundaries and [EcosystemMapProposal.md](EcosystemMapProposal.md). Tasks 1–2 map to proposal integration/transport/server-support requirements, Integration contracts 1/6/8 and [EnvironmentalAcquisition.md](EnvironmentalAcquisition.md#payload-and-routing); task 3 maps to proposal persistence/coverage/lifecycle, Integration contract 7 and [EnvironmentalStorage.md](EnvironmentalStorage.md#ordering-and-lifetime). Tasks 4–5 map to proposal performance/validation expectations and [HabitatEvaluation.md](HabitatEvaluation.md#native-cpu-profiling). Tasks 6–7 map to proposal player-interaction/validation expectations, Integration contract 4 and HabitatEvaluation's coverage/availability/lifecycle contracts. Tasks 8–9 map to the complete proposal and current [SpeciesCatalogue.md](SpeciesCatalogue.md), [EnvironmentalAcquisition.md](EnvironmentalAcquisition.md), [EnvironmentalStorage.md](EnvironmentalStorage.md) and [HabitatEvaluation.md](HabitatEvaluation.md). Each linked architecture document was consulted; no unread controlling document is substituted with a summary.

Fresh subagent validation: with `VINTAGE_STORY=G:/Vintagestory`, `NUGET_PACKAGES=C:/Users/Sisco/.nuget/packages` and game Lib on PATH, `dotnet test VanillaExpanded.Tests/VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore -p:UseSharedCompilation=false --filter FullyQualifiedName~EcosystemMap` passes 99 tests; the same command without the filter passes 2,165. Canonical `./build.ps1 --configuration=Release` passes JSON validation/build/package. An initial native-profiler import ambiguity was corrected before these final passing checks; existing unrelated C# and Cake Pkcs warnings remain. Final cache-fix ZIP `Releases/vanillaexpanded_999.99.9.zip`: 1,980,898 bytes, SHA-256 `6251B0FC77777D6895EE736530DADE4A8A241674E4457F67F9E24134C7BA79BF`. Packaged DLL matches Release output (SHA-256 `E585E2C15A7C8635A8F7B3CF2BF5F702F92D93208ABD213BA771E6842EEFB97E`); shaders, both localization domains, species-group patch and manifest match source. Installed assembly/product versions and Integration contract 1's Lib/Essentials hashes were reverified.

Packaging/source evidence: packaged registration, layer, session and server shrub owners coexist with unrelated feature systems. Manifest stays Universal with `requiredOnServer: false`; optional reset sends require confirmed channel support. Routing tests exercise the installed envelope, inactive-layer receipt, original-array persistence, same-connection purge reacquisition and unsupported-server reset suppression. No backfill callback sends a retrieval request. Lifecycle tests cover independent SQLite reopen, partial/versioned records, world/dimension identities, accepted-write shutdown durability, purge provenance, normal packet/unload adapters, targeted invalidation, coverage changes and unchanged sentinel bytes in a test-only vanilla-map path. These results do not establish live unrelated-feature gameplay without server support or every live lifecycle transition.

Performance investigation: the user supplied fresh Debug-launch logs and an affected-view capture, explicitly requesting logs first. Log analysis preceded replay. `client-debug.log:25–26` verifies the worktree `bin/Debug/Mods/mod/VanillaExpanded.dll` was loaded. `client-main.log` (273,832 bytes, modified 2026-10-20 13:00:27 UTC) contains 456 logged slow frames, 261 with an ecosystem range at 05:58:04–05:58:37 local log time. These are conditional samples from native slow-frame logging, not whole-session averages or GPU timings. Independent analysis reproduces the values below; no ecosystem exception explains the lag.

| Logged CPU range | Median | Mean | Maximum |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Ecosystem total | 84.16 ms | 89.34 ms | 368.18 ms |
| Drawing/restoration | 46.52 ms | 46.26 ms | 83.15 ms |
| Texture acquisition | 25.05 ms | 27.83 ms | 74.35 ms |
| View membership | 0.84 ms | 5.20 ms | 319.07 ms |

The input-preparation, placement and explored-coverage medians are 5.09, 2.44 and 0.82 ms. Texture acquisition includes cache lookup and possible uploads; draw elapsed time includes CPU submission and possible driver waits. Those marks alone do not isolate original GPU execution. Native profiler hooks remain opt-in and do not enable logging/readbacks themselves.

Fresh `C:/Users/Sisco/Desktop/ecosystem-capture.rdc`: 1,911,849,986 bytes, modified 2026-10-20 12:57:06 UTC. This frame precedes the later log interval and is not asserted to be the exact same view/frame. Replay identifies program `ResourceId::137380` from captured habitat shader source. It records exactly 7,510 ecosystem draws (events 91902–210012) under one activation, with two matrix uploads, five sampler resets and no in-pass texture upload. Regional bindings recur in 1,083 groups across 86 distinct regional owners. Sampled input textures comprise 86 each for climate/forest/shrubs and 7,510 each for heights/coverage: 15,278 texture objects, 23,533,252 raw payload bytes (about 22.44 MiB; excludes driver overhead, unused cached textures and other game resources). The actual frame uploads 254 height textures, 254 coverage textures and three textures for one region before pass activation, leaving 14,767 sampled textures already present. This is partial population, not wholesale tile regeneration. Counter-instrumented replay reports 185.21 ms summed ecosystem event GPU durations and 1,394.98 ms across the frame; replay/query instrumentation and workload differences prevent treating these as original live GPU frame times or proof of a shader bottleneck.

A targeted, TEMP-only CPU experiment linked the exact production LRU source, protecting the oldest half before shrinking to half. Three timed samples after warm-up in each configuration verified protected identities and final bounds. The original source performs exactly `h*(h+1)` protection checks for half-size `h`; the updated candidate index performs `1.5*N` checks. This establishes a concrete quadratic shrink path, while not proving the live 319 ms spike had identical cache size/order or was entirely caused by it.

| Initial entries | Debug shrink before → after | Release shrink before → after | Protection checks before → after |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 5,000 | 24.57 → 0.64 ms | 9.61 → 0.56 ms | 6,252,500 → 7,500 |
| 10,000 | 97.90 → 1.22 ms | 40.72 → 0.90 ms | 25,005,000 → 15,000 |
| 20,000 | 403.89 → 2.39 ms | 160.14 → 1.89 ms | 100,010,000 → 30,000 |

The cache fix indexes off-view eviction candidates once per working-set change, retains protected inputs and preserves the existing resource owners. Mutable source validity still receives an eviction-time check/fallback, so the complexity claim is limited to ordinary stable predicates. Rebuilding candidate nodes increases measured shrink allocation by approximately `24*N` bytes (20,000-entry case about 342,480 → 822,480 bytes, with small runtime noise). This tradeoff removes the measured repeated scan without introducing a CPU habitat grid or changing shader/range semantics. Deterministic operation-count/identity/recency/dynamic-protection regressions pass. The experiment project was removed safely; no standalone graphics harness or permanent readback diagnostic was introduced.

Second source review and independent affected-code audit found no confirmed protection, capacity, coverage, graphics-state or source-lifetime regression. Final focused/full/build/package receipts above apply to the cache-fix candidate; both habitat shaders are unchanged. The narrow fix has synthetic before/after evidence but no live post-fix acceptance yet. Serious sustained drawing/acquisition costs remain unresolved; batching input storage/submissions is the next rendering investigation, not a completed optimization. Representative map opening, panning, selection, capture/delivery throughput and complete CPU/GPU metadata accounting also remain required. Mixed-coverage appearance/input-arrival, minimap/inactive-tab exclusion, old uncached areas and live lifecycle acceptance retain their separate gates. No game launch or custom installation was performed on the user's behalf. All Phase 10 and final-acceptance markers remain unchecked.

## Final acceptance

- [ ] Every proposal requirement is mapped to completed work above; only the explicitly deferred supplemental server catalogue remains outside scope.
- [ ] Persistent environmental data survives normal lifecycle transitions; disposable work can be discarded; explicit purge clears feature-owned state.
- [ ] Full-map-only suitable/unavailable presentation, species scope, original shrub delivery, separate storage, and no-backfill behavior meet the proposal.
- [ ] Linked documentation and checklist reflect verified completion without overstating source inspection, automated checks, or live-runtime evidence.
